---
/**
 * The four moves a person makes on a hard question, struck out — and the one
 * move the machine has instead.
 *
 * The teaching is in the shape of the page rather than in any single label. The
 * top band is everything the reader would naturally assume a model can do when
 * it is stuck; every one of those boxes carries a diagonal strike, and the
 * sentence underneath says in words why. The bottom band has exactly one box in
 * it. A reader who counts the boxes has already had the argument.
 *
 * The closing pair of lines is the part that stops the picture being merely
 * gloomy: the struck-out moves do come back, and writing them down is what buys
 * them. That is the whole of `reasoning-models` in one sentence.
 *
 * Nothing is carried by colour (hard rule 9). The unavailable moves are marked
 * by a diagonal line across the box — a shape — and named as unavailable in the
 * sentence below the row. The two-column table is read by position and by its
 * headings, not by tint.
 *
 * The three rows of the table are the arithmetic from `forward-pass`, restated
 * one line at a time: a model produces one token per complete sweep, so writing
 * nine hundred tokens of working before answering is nine hundred sweeps that
 * answering immediately would not have bought.
 *
 * Decorative by itself, so `aria-hidden`: `Figure` supplies the accessible name
 * and the description.
 */
---

<svg viewBox="0 0 640 388" class="w-full" aria-hidden="true">
  <text x="16" y="24" class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="8"
    >WHAT YOU DO WITH A QUESTION YOU CANNOT ANSWER AT ONCE</text
  >

  <!-- The four moves. Same box, same strike, four times: the repetition is the
       point, because the reader is meant to look for the one that survives. -->
  <g class="fill-paper-sunken stroke-rule" stroke-width="1.25">
    <rect x="16" y="36" width="146" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
    <rect x="174" y="36" width="146" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
    <rect x="332" y="36" width="146" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
    <rect x="490" y="36" width="146" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink-muted" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">
    <text x="89" y="60">read the question</text>
    <text x="89" y="74">again, from the top</text>

    <text x="247" y="60">try one approach,</text>
    <text x="247" y="74">watch it fail, back out</text>

    <text x="405" y="60">sit with it a while</text>
    <text x="405" y="74">before saying anything</text>

    <text x="563" y="60">check the answer</text>
    <text x="563" y="74">before it leaves you</text>
  </g>

  <!-- Struck through, not tinted out: the mark survives greyscale and print. -->
  <g class="stroke-rule-strong" stroke-width="1.5">
    <line x1="26" y1="84" x2="152" y2="44"></line>
    <line x1="184" y1="84" x2="310" y2="44"></line>
    <line x1="342" y1="84" x2="468" y2="44"></line>
    <line x1="500" y1="84" x2="626" y2="44"></line>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink" font-size="10.5">
    <text x="16" y="112"
      >Not one of these exists inside a single sweep. The stack has a set number
      of stops,</text
    >
    <text x="16" y="128"
      >it visits each of them once, and no wire anywhere points backwards.</text
    >
  </g>

  <line x1="16" y1="146" x2="624" y2="146" class="stroke-rule" stroke-width="1"
  ></line>

  <text x="16" y="166" class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="8"
    >WHAT THE MACHINE HAS INSTEAD — ALL OF IT</text
  >

  <defs>
    <marker
      id="lever-arrow"
      viewBox="0 0 10 10"
      refX="9"
      refY="5"
      markerWidth="5"
      markerHeight="5"
      orient="auto"
    >
      <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" class="fill-ink-muted"></path>
    </marker>
  </defs>

  <rect
    x="16"
    y="178"
    width="204"
    height="56"
    rx="6"
    class="fill-paper-raised stroke-accent"
    stroke-width="2"></rect>
  <text
    x="118"
    y="211"
    class="fill-ink font-display"
    font-size="13"
    font-weight="700"
    text-anchor="middle">produce one more token</text
  >

  <line
    x1="228"
    y1="206"
    x2="266"
    y2="206"
    class="stroke-ink-muted"
    stroke-width="1.25"
    marker-end="url(#lever-arrow)"></line>

  <rect
    x="276"
    y="178"
    width="348"
    height="56"
    rx="6"
    class="fill-paper-sunken stroke-rule-strong"
    stroke-width="1.25"></rect>
  <g class="fill-ink-muted" font-size="10">
    <text x="292" y="198">one more complete sweep through every layer —</text>
    <text x="292" y="212">the same sweep, at the same price as any other —</text
    >
    <text x="292" y="226"
      >and it reads everything written so far, its own words included</text
    >
  </g>

  <line x1="320" y1="248" x2="320" y2="342" class="stroke-rule" stroke-width="1"
  ></line>

  <g class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="8">
    <text x="300" y="262" text-anchor="end">TOKENS OF WORKING FIRST</text>
    <text x="340" y="262">PASSES THROUGH THE MACHINE</text>
  </g>

  <g
    class="fill-ink font-display"
    font-size="13"
    font-weight="700"
    text-anchor="end"
  >
    <text x="300" y="290">none</text>
    <text x="300" y="312">40</text>
    <text x="300" y="334">900</text>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink font-display" font-size="13" font-weight="700">
    <text x="340" y="290">1</text>
    <text x="340" y="312">41</text>
    <text x="340" y="334">901</text>
  </g>

  <g
    class="fill-ink font-display"
    font-size="12"
    font-weight="700"
    text-anchor="middle"
  >
    <text x="320" y="362"
      >Every move in the top row comes back the moment it is written down —</text
    >
    <text x="320" y="378">and writing it down is the only way to buy it.</text>
  </g>
</svg>
